---
import '../../styles/review-site.css';
import '../../styles/research-lab.css';
import '../../styles/ember-research-system.css';
import '../../styles/ember-surface-framework.css';
import HearthNavigation from '../../components/HearthNavigation.astro';
import ResearchProjectFrame from '../../components/ResearchProjectFrame.astro';
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow" />
    <meta name="description" content="Local review candidate for Exocore, a proposed person-owned knowledge workbench." />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#171311" />
    <title>Exocore · Hearth &amp; Code review candidate</title>
  </head>
  <body class="research-body research-body--review review-body ember-surface">
    <a class="research-skip" href="#main-content">Skip to the project</a>
    <HearthNavigation current="portfolio" context="Exocore / local project review" />
    <main id="main-content" class="research-main">
      <ResearchProjectFrame
        eyebrow="Research project / person-owned knowledge workbench"
        title="Exocore"
        summary="Exocore is the proposed workbench taking shape inside Hearth &amp; Code: a person-owned place for projects, questions, sources, choices, and bounded AI help. This page describes the public orientation shell; it does not project capabilities from separate private implementation work onto that shell."
        status="Candidate · project orientation"
        facts={[
          { label: 'Project type', value: 'Knowledge-work infrastructure' },
          { label: 'Public surface', value: 'Orientation shell' },
          { label: 'Authority', value: 'Person-owned decisions and release' },
          { label: 'Evidence boundary', value: 'Implementation and use require their own records' },
        ]}
        sections={[
          { id: 'exocore-purpose', label: 'Purpose' },
          { id: 'exocore-shape', label: 'First-version principles' },
          { id: 'exocore-state', label: 'Current boundary' },
        ]}
        returnHref="/research/"
        returnLabel="Return to Research"
      >
        <div class="review-page">
          <section id="exocore-purpose" class="review-section" aria-labelledby="exocore-purpose-title">
            <div class="review-section-head">
              <div>
                <p class="review-kicker">The purpose</p>
                <h2 id="exocore-purpose-title">A workbench should make the work easier to find again.</h2>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="review-story-panel">
              <p>Exocore begins with a simple frustration: important work can disappear into a pile of tabs, notes, half-made plans, and the private effort of remembering where the thought was going. The proposed workbench tries to keep enough context visible that a person can return without rebuilding the whole mental scene from scratch.</p>
              <p>It does not aim to become an autonomous authority. The person decides what matters, what gets acted on, what remains private, and when an answer is good enough to keep.</p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section id="exocore-shape" class="review-section" aria-labelledby="exocore-shape-title">
            <div class="review-section-head">
              <div>
                <p class="review-kicker">The shape of the first version</p>
                <h2 id="exocore-shape-title">Three useful promises, held lightly.</h2>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="review-card-grid">
              <article class="review-card">
                <p class="review-label">Keep the thread</p>
                <h3>Records that remember the state of the work.</h3>
                <p>Projects, decisions, sources, and unfinished questions should stay legible enough that a person can recover a complicated line of thought after a break.</p>
              </article>
              <article class="review-card">
                <p class="review-label">Make the source visible</p>
                <h3>Help that can be checked.</h3>
                <p>Useful AI assistance should leave a trail: what it used, what it suggested, and where a person can challenge, correct, or discard it.</p>
              </article>
              <article class="review-card">
                <p class="review-label">Keep authority human</p>
                <h3>Support without a quiet handoff of judgment.</h3>
                <p>The workbench may sort, retrieve, draft, or propose. It should stop before consequential decisions, outward-facing claims, and final interpretation.</p>
              </article>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section id="exocore-state" class="review-section" aria-labelledby="exocore-now-title">
            <div class="review-split">
              <div>
                <p class="review-kicker">What this public surface establishes</p>
                <h2 id="exocore-now-title">An orientation to the project, not a runtime demonstration.</h2>
                <p>The linked public repository contains an early desktop shell and a plain orientation to the project. It demonstrates a beginning and sets a boundary around what does not yet run on that public surface. Private implementation history elsewhere in the studio is a separate record; it does not establish that those capabilities are present here.</p>
                <div class="review-actions">
                  <a class="review-button is-primary" href="https://github.com/hearthandcode/exocore-platform" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Inspect Exocore on GitHub ↗</a>
                  <a class="review-button" href="/research/">Read the research overview</a>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="review-kicker">What remains open</p>
                <ul class="review-mini-list">
                  <li><strong>Can it stay simple?</strong>A workbench that adds too much cognitive overhead has missed the point.</li>
                  <li><strong>Can it stay honest?</strong>Every helpful action needs a boundary a person can see and revise.</li>
                  <li><strong>Can it become useful?</strong>Only lived use can answer that, and one person’s answer is still only one person’s answer.</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>
        </div>
      </ResearchProjectFrame>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
